== Affections Not Made by Ourselves

IV. It is no sign that affections are gracious, or that they are otherwise, that persons did not make them themselves, or stir them up by their own contriving and their own strength.

There are many in these days who condemn all affections which are stirred up in a way the persons who have them cannot account for, affections that do not seem to be the fruit of any of their own efforts, or the natural result of the faculties and principles of human nature in such circumstances and under such means, but seem to come from the influence of some outside and supernatural power on their minds. How greatly the doctrine of the inward experience, or felt perception, of the immediate power and working of the Spirit of God has been reproached and ridiculed by many lately! They say the manner of the Spirit of God is to work together with the use of means and with our own efforts in a silent, secret and undiscernible way, so that there is no distinguishing by sense between the influences of the Spirit of God and the natural workings of the faculties of our own minds.

And it is true that for anyone to expect to receive the saving influences of the Spirit of God while he neglects a diligent use of the appointed means of grace is unreasonable presumption. And to expect that the Spirit of God will work savingly on their minds without the Spirit making use of means, as serving toward the effect, is enthusiastic. It is also undoubtedly true that the Spirit of God is very varied in the manner and circumstances of his working, and that at times he works in a more secret and gradual way, and from smaller beginnings, than at others.

But suppose there is indeed a power, entirely different from and beyond our power, or the power of all means and instruments, and above the power of nature, which is required to produce saving grace in the heart, as the country generally professes. Then it is certainly not unreasonable in any way to suppose that this effect should very often be produced in such a way as to make it very plain, apparent and perceptible that it is so. If grace is indeed owing to the powerful and efficacious working of an outside agent, or a divine efficient cause outside ourselves, why is it unreasonable to suppose that it should seem so to those who experience it? Is it a strange thing that it should seem to be as it is? Grace in the heart is in fact not produced by our strength, nor is it the effect of the natural power of our own faculties or of any means or instruments, but it is properly the workmanship and production of the Spirit of the Almighty. When that is so, is it a strange and unaccountable thing that it should seem so to those who experience it, in agreement with the truth and not directly contrary to the truth? And if persons tell of effects they are conscious of in their own minds which seem to them to come not from the natural power or working of their minds but from the supernatural power of some other agent, should that at once be looked on as a sure evidence that they are under a delusion, because things seem to them to be as they are? For this is the objection that is made. It is looked on as clear evidence that the apprehensions and affections many persons have are not really from such a cause, because they seem to them to be from that cause. They declare that what they are conscious of plainly seems to them to be not from themselves but from the mighty power of the Spirit of God. And others condemn them for this, and decide that what they experience is not from the Spirit of God but from themselves, or from the devil. This is how unreasonably multitudes are treated by their neighbors at this day.

The Scripture abundantly teaches that grace in the soul is so much the effect of God’s power that it is fitly compared to those effects which are furthest from being owing to any strength in the one who receives them: such as #emph[generation,] or #emph[being begotten;] and #emph[resurrection,] or #emph[being raised from the dead;] and #emph[creation,] or #emph[being brought out of nothing into being.] It teaches that it is an effect in which the mighty power of God is greatly glorified and the exceeding greatness of his power is shown.#footnote[Eph 1:17–20.] If it is indeed so, then what account can be given of the Almighty, in so great a work of his power, hiding his power so carefully that those who experience it should be able to discern nothing of it? Or what reason or revelation does anyone have to decide that he does so? If we may judge by the Scripture, this does not agree with God’s manner in his works and dispensations. On the contrary, it is God’s manner, in the great works of power and mercy which he works for his people, to order things so as to make his hand visible, his power conspicuous, and men’s dependence on him most evident. He does it so that no flesh should glory in his presence,#footnote[1 Cor 1:27–29.] so that God alone might be exalted,#footnote[Isa 2:11–17.] so that the excellence of the power might be from God and not from man,#footnote[2 Cor 4:7.] so that Christ’s power might be shown in our weakness,#footnote[2 Cor 12:9.] and so that no one might say, “My own hand has saved me.”#footnote[Judg 7:2.] So it was in most of the temporal salvations God worked for #emph[Israel] of old, which were types of the salvation of God’s people from their spiritual enemies. So it was in the redemption of #emph[Israel] from their bondage in #emph[Egypt.] He redeemed them with a strong hand and an outstretched arm, and so that his power might be the more conspicuous, he allowed #emph[Israel] first to be brought into the most helpless and forlorn circumstances. So it was in the great deliverance by #emph[Gideon.] God would have his army reduced to a handful, and they with no other weapons than trumpets and torches and clay jars. So it was in the deliverance of #emph[Israel] from #emph[Goliath] by a youth with a sling and a stone. So it was in that great work of God, his calling of the #emph[Gentiles] and converting of the heathen world after Christ’s ascension. This came after the world by its wisdom did not know God, and all the efforts of philosophers to reform the world had proved useless for many ages, and it had become abundantly evident by everything that the world was utterly helpless by anything but the mighty power of God. And so it was in most of the conversions of particular persons of which we have an account in the history of the New Testament. They were not worked on in the silent, secret, gradual and imperceptible way that is now insisted on, but with those plain evidences of a supernatural power, wonderfully and suddenly causing a great change, which in these days are looked on as certain signs of delusion and enthusiasm.

The apostle in Ephesians 1:18–19 speaks of God’s enlightening the minds of Christians, and so bringing them to believe in Christ, to this end, that they #emph[might know] the exceeding greatness of his power toward those who believe. The words are, “I ask that the eyes of your#super[+] heart may be enlightened, so that you#super[+] may know the hope of His calling, the riches of His glorious inheritance in the saints, and the surpassing greatness of His power to us who believe. These are in accordance with the working of His mighty strength,” and so on. Now when the apostle speaks of their being the subjects of his power in this way, in their enlightening and effectual calling, to the end that they might know what his mighty power was toward those who believe, he can mean nothing else than #emph[that they might know it by experience.] But if the saints know this power by experience, then they feel it and discern it and are conscious of it, as something perceptibly distinguishable from the natural workings of their own minds. And this does not agree with the notion of God working so secretly and undiscernibly that it cannot be known that they are under the influence of any outside power at all, except as they may argue it from the assertions of Scripture, which is a different thing from knowing it by experience.

So it is very unreasonable and unscriptural to decide that affections are not from the gracious working of God’s Spirit because they perceptibly do not come from the persons themselves who have them.

On the other hand, it is no evidence that affections are gracious that they are not purposely produced by those who have them, or that they arise in their minds in a way they cannot account for.

There are some who make this an argument in their own favor. When speaking of what they have experienced, they say, “I am sure I did not make it myself. It was the fruit of no contriving or effort of mine. It came when I was thinking nothing of it. If I could have the world for it, I cannot make it again when I please.” And from this they decide that what they have experienced must be from the mighty influence of the Spirit of God and is of a saving nature. But they do so very ignorantly and without grounds. What they have experienced may indeed not be from themselves directly, but may be from the working of an invisible agent, some spirit besides their own. But it does not follow from this that it was from the Spirit of God. There are other spirits besides the Holy Spirit who have influence on the minds of men. We are told not to believe every spirit, but to test the spirits to see whether they are from God. There are many false spirits, exceedingly busy with men, who often disguise themselves as angels of light and, in many wonderful ways, with great subtlety and power, imitate the workings of the Spirit of God. And there are many of #emph[Satan’s] workings which are very distinguishable from the voluntary exercises of men’s own minds. They are so in the dreadful and horrid suggestions and blasphemous thoughts he thrusts in, with which he pursues many persons, and in the vain and fruitless frights and terrors of which he is the author. And the power of #emph[Satan] may be as immediate and as evident in false comforts and joys as in terrors and horrid suggestions, and in fact it often is. It is not in men’s power to put themselves into such raptures as the #emph[Anabaptists] in #emph[Germany,] and many other raving enthusiasts like them, have experienced.

And besides, it must be considered that persons may have impressions on their minds which may not be of their own producing, nor from an evil spirit, but from the Spirit of God, and yet not be from any saving influence of the Spirit of God, but only a common one. And those who have such impressions may be among those we read of in Heb 6:4–5, “those who have once been enlightened, who have tasted the heavenly gift, who have shared in the Holy Spirit, who have tasted the goodness of the word of God and the powers of the coming age,” and yet may be wholly unacquainted with those #emph[better things that accompany salvation] spoken of in verse 9.

And where neither a good nor an evil spirit has any immediate hand, persons may have strange apprehensions and imaginations, and strong affections accompanying them, arising in a way they cannot account for, which they do not produce voluntarily. This is especially so with those of a weak and nervous constitution of body, whose brain is weak and easily receives impressions. We see that such persons are liable to such impressions about temporal things, and there is equal reason why they should be about spiritual things. As a person who is asleep has dreams of which he is not the voluntary author, so such persons may in the same way have involuntary impressions when they are awake.
